/**

@page page_exahype_programmers_guide_new_solver Introduce a new solver

\tableofcontents

This page walks through what it takes to add a genuinely new solver to
ExaHyPE 2, i.e. a new discretisation (or a new variant of an existing one)
rather than a new PDE solved with an existing discretisation. Modelling the
*what* of a PDE (unknowns, PDE terms) against an existing solver is covered
on the @ref page_exahype_modelling_pde_solver "modelling the PDE solver" page
and does not require anything below. This page is for the *how*.


# The four building blocks

An exahype2.api.Solver only holds the descriptive, discretisation-independent
part of a solver (name, unknowns, auxiliary variables, PDE terms). It does
not know how to turn that description into C++. That is delegated to four
abstract building blocks, all nested classes of exahype2.api.Solver
(`src/exahype2/api/Solver.py`), each of which a concrete discretisation has
to subclass and assign an instance of onto the solver:

- **Solver.NumericalScheme** - the actual discretisation. Implements
  `construct_output(exahype2_project, peano_project, time_step)`, which adds
  the discretisation's tasks (reconstruct, update, project onto faces, ...)
  into the time-stepping task graph. Assigned via `solver.numerical_scheme`.
- **Solver.GridConstruction** - how the mesh this solver runs on is built and
  kept up to date. Implements `construct_output(exahype2_project,
  peano_project, setup_simulation)`, adding tasks into the setup-phase task
  graph. Assigned via `solver.grid_construction`.
- **Solver.Plotter** - how the solution is written out. Implements
  `construct_output(exahype2_project, peano_project, plot)`. Assigned via
  `solver.plotter` (optional - a solver without a plotter is valid).
- **Solver.SolverGenerator** - turns the solver description into the
  Abstract*.h/.cpp glue classes (`construct_output(exahype2_project,
  peano_project)`). Solvers typically reuse the generic implementation rather
  than provide their own.

`Solver.generate_solver_realisation()` asserts that `grid_construction` and
`numerical_scheme` are set before it calls into any of them, so both are
mandatory; only `plotter` is optional.


# Reference implementation to copy from

Rather than starting from a blank page, base a new discretisation on the
Finite Volumes / Rusanov implementation under `src/exahype2/api/fv/`, which
exercises all four building blocks against the task-graph architecture:

- `RusanovWithExplicitEuler.py` - Solver.NumericalScheme. Creates the patch
  and patch-overlap mesh data in its constructor (@see
  `__create_data_structures()`), wires up the storage generators and mesh
  data repository (@see `__add_data_to_repository()`), and assembles the
  time-stepping task graph (reconstruction, cell update, CFL condition,
  project on faces, compute time step size).
- `SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid.py` / `SetupSimulationWithUserCodedRefinementCriterion.py`
  - two Solver.GridConstruction variants: a mesh that is refined once and
  then stays regular, versus one refined adaptively every sweep by a
  user-coded criterion.
- `PlotAsVTKFile.py` - Solver.Plotter, maps the patch data onto a plot mesh
  and writes it as VTK.

A new discretisation typically needs its own patch/data layout and its own
task graph in the Solver.NumericalScheme subclass, but can often reuse the
existing Solver.GridConstruction and Solver.Plotter variants unchanged if its
data still fits the same patch-based layout.


# Wiring it into a project

The user-facing Python script that exercises a solver assigns the three
building blocks and hands the solver to an exahype2.api.Project, e.g. (see
`benchmarks/exahype2/euler/smooth-convergence-test/smooth-convergence-test.py`
for the full, working example):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
thesolver = exahype2.api.Solver(solver_name, unknowns, auxiliary_variables)
thesolver.add_pde_term(exahype2.api.Flux(stateless=False))
thesolver.add_pde_term(exahype2.api.MaxEigenvalue(stateless=False))

thesolver.numerical_scheme = exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler(thesolver, patch_size)
thesolver.grid_construction = exahype2.api.fv.SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid(thesolver, volume_size=...)
thesolver.plotter = exahype2.api.fv.PlotAsVTKFile(thesolver)

project.add_solver(thesolver)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

`project.generate_Peano4_project()` then calls
`Solver.generate_solver_realisation()` on every registered solver, which in
turn calls each building block's `construct_output()` in sequence
(`solver_generator`, then `grid_construction`, then `numerical_scheme`, then
`plotter` if set).

<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
This task-graph-based exahype2.api is under active development. Some of the
building blocks referenced here (patch data layout, storage generators) are
still being ported over from the legacy `python/exahype2/solvers` API, so
check `git log` on the files above before relying on details not covered on
this page.
</div>

*/
